::GEO_U01_L01_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L01_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A diagram shows points A, B, and C on one straight line, with B between A and C. Which conclusion is supported by the diagram? {
=A, B, and C are collinear. #Correct. Points shown on the same straight line are collinear, so this conclusion is supported by the diagram.
~AB = BC #This assumes the two parts have equal length. The diagram would need labels or matching tick marks to support equal segment lengths.
~Angle ABC is a right angle. #This adds angle information that is not shown. The diagram describes collinear points, not a square corner mark or angle measure.
~A and C are the same point. #This misunderstands the diagram. A and C are different labeled points on the same line.
}

::GEO_U01_L01_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L01_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A diagram shows segment DE and segment FG with matching tick marks. What do the markings show? {
~DE and FG are parallel. #Tick marks on segments show congruence, not parallel lines. Parallel markings are usually arrow marks on lines.
=DE = FG #Correct. Matching tick marks show congruent segments, so the segment lengths are equal.
~D, E, F, and G are collinear. #The tick marks do not show that all four points lie on one line. Collinearity requires the points to be on the same straight line.
~DE is longer than FG. #Matching tick marks show equal lengths, not one segment being longer than the other.
}

::GEO_U01_L01_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L01_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A drawing shows two angles that look equal, but there are no angle measures or matching arc marks. What can be concluded? {
~The angles are congruent because they look equal. #This relies on appearance alone. Geometry conclusions need evidence from markings, measures, given information, or valid reasoning.
~Each angle measures 90&deg;. #A right angle conclusion requires a square corner mark or a stated measure of 90&deg;.
=The angles cannot be concluded congruent from appearance alone. #Correct. Without measures, matching arc marks, or a given statement, congruence is not supported.
~The angles are supplementary. #Supplementary angles require evidence that their measures add to 180&deg;. No such evidence is given.
}

::GEO_U01_L01_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L01_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A diagram shows angle RST with a square corner mark at S. Which statement is supported? {
~m&angle;RST = 45&deg; #A square corner mark does not show 45&deg;. It shows a right angle.
=m&angle;RST = 90&deg; #Correct. A square corner mark indicates a right angle, and a right angle measures 90&deg;.
~RS = ST #A square corner mark gives information about the angle, not the lengths of the sides.
~R, S, and T are collinear. #A right angle means the rays form a corner, not one straight line.
}

::GEO_U01_L01_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L01_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A student says two lines are parallel because they look like they never meet. What is the best correction? {
~The lines must be parallel if they are drawn close together. #Closeness does not prove parallel lines. Parallel conclusions need markings, a given statement, or valid reasoning.
~The lines cannot be parallel if no numbers are shown. #Numbers are not the only evidence. Parallel markings or a given statement can also support the conclusion.
~The lines are perpendicular because they do not touch. #Perpendicular lines meet to form right angles. The student's issue is assuming parallelism from appearance.
=Parallel lines require markings, a given statement, or another valid reason. #Correct. Appearance alone is not enough evidence for a Geometry conclusion.
}
